1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
So what happens if I go to Eretz

2
00:00:02,140 --> 00:00:04,759
Yisrael and I follow the psak of the

3
00:00:04,759 --> 00:00:06,660
Beit Yosef, which is also the psak of

4
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:08,359
the Mishnah Berurah, which was also the psak

5
00:00:08,359 --> 00:00:10,460
of the Arukh HaShulchan? It was the psak

6
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:12,859
of the consensus for many, many years until

7
00:00:12,859 --> 00:00:15,339
recently when it became more popular to follow

8
00:00:15,339 --> 00:00:17,019
the psak of the Chakham Tzvi, who says

9
00:00:17,019 --> 00:00:18,600
you only keep one day when you're visiting

10
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:20,579
Eretz Yisrael. Let's say I'm of the opinion

11
00:00:20,579 --> 00:00:22,219
that I should be keeping two days of

12
00:00:22,219 --> 00:00:24,359
Yom Tov in Eretz Yisrael because I'm legitimately

13
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:26,920
a Ben Chutz LaAretz. I'm not somebody who

14
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:29,160
counts myself as a Ben Eretz Yisrael as

15
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:30,219
much as I would want to be there

16
00:00:30,219 --> 00:00:31,839
right now. The bottom line is I'm not.

17
00:00:32,259 --> 00:00:33,579
So the question would become: Would I be

18
00:00:33,579 --> 00:00:35,539
allowed to ask someone who lives in Eretz

19
00:00:35,539 --> 00:00:38,159
Yisrael legitimately, would I be allowed to ask

20
00:00:38,159 --> 00:00:40,493
them to do melakhah for me on Yom

21
00:00:40,493 --> 00:00:43,979
Tov Sheni? Or is that considered similar to

22
00:00:43,979 --> 00:00:46,939
Amira LeNokhri? The Shulchan Arukh says the same

23
00:00:46,939 --> 00:00:49,340
way Amira LeNokhri is not permissible on Shabbos

24
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:50,780
or Yom Tov, just like we're not allowed

25
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:52,310
to ask a non-Jew on Shabbos or Yom

26
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:54,259
Tov to do anything for us, so too

27
00:00:54,259 --> 00:00:55,659
we are not allowed to ask on Yom

28
00:00:55,659 --> 00:00:57,759
Tov Sheni. Yom Tov Sheni is just as

29
00:00:57,759 --> 00:01:00,390
serious almost as a regular day of Yom

30
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:02,240
Tov. Even though it's only rabbinic, even though

31
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:05,299
it's only miderabbanan, the prohibition of Amira LeNokhri

32
00:01:05,659 --> 00:01:08,299
applies equally on Yom Tov Rishon as it

33
00:01:08,299 --> 00:01:10,519
does on Yom Tov Sheni. So would it

34
00:01:10,519 --> 00:01:13,299
be the same to ask a Jew who

35
00:01:13,299 --> 00:01:14,819
is not observing Yom Tov? That's a strange

36
00:01:14,819 --> 00:01:16,659
case. Would I be allowed to ask a

37
00:01:16,659 --> 00:01:18,700
Jew who's not observing Yom Tov to do

38
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,299
something for me? What would be another application

39
00:01:21,299 --> 00:01:23,386
of that? Anybody know? Forget about Yom Tov

40
00:01:23,386 --> 00:01:28,319
Sheni for a minute. Let's say in the

41
00:01:28,319 --> 00:01:32,099
summer months when I'm mekabel Shabbos early. So

42
00:01:32,099 --> 00:01:34,340
I'm mekabel Shabbos at 5:30 but Shabbos really

43
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:37,219
doesn't come in until 7:45. So I'm in

44
00:01:37,219 --> 00:01:38,700
a position where I'm able to be mekabel

45
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:40,120
Shabbos early but there are many of my

46
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,560
neighbors and friends who come home later from

47
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:44,780
work, have younger children, whatever it is, they're

48
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:46,640
going to be mekabbel Shabbos at the zman

49
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:49,859
and not early. So the question is during

50
00:01:49,859 --> 00:01:51,599
that interim period, if let's say I come

51
00:01:51,599 --> 00:01:53,500
home from shul and I already davened Friday

52
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:55,280
night, I said Mizmor Shir, I was mekabbel

53
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:57,599
Shabbos upon myself earlier than I had to,

54
00:01:58,040 --> 00:01:59,519
and now I realize I forgot to turn

55
00:01:59,519 --> 00:02:00,980
on the air conditioner, I forgot to turn

56
00:02:00,980 --> 00:02:02,659
on the lights, would I be allowed to

57
00:02:02,659 --> 00:02:05,560
ask a neighbor to do that for me

58
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:08,199
when that neighbor has not yet accepted Shabbos

59
00:02:08,199 --> 00:02:11,020
upon him or herself? That's a similar kind

60
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:12,759
of question. It gets a little bit more

61
00:02:12,759 --> 00:02:14,740
complicated. Let's say on Shabbos I do not

62
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:17,219
open bottles, right? There's a big machlokes whether

63
00:02:17,219 --> 00:02:18,780
you're allowed to open bottles on Shabbos not

64
00:02:18,780 --> 00:02:21,099
so clear. Let's say my family minhag is

65
00:02:21,099 --> 00:02:22,460
that you're not allowed to open bottles. What's

66
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,520
the issue? Big question. Some say it might

67
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:26,800
be makeh bepatish, whatever. It may be a

68
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,620
violation of a melakhah. It's not clear exactly

69
00:02:28,620 --> 00:02:30,020
what it is, but it's a big discussion.

70
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:32,120
Some say it's totally fine, some say it's

71
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,639
totally not. So if you're from the group

72
00:02:34,639 --> 00:02:36,000
of people who say that you're not allowed

73
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,419
to, but I have someone at my table

74
00:02:37,419 --> 00:02:40,340
who legitimately follows their psak, which is that

75
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:42,199
they are allowed to, would I be allowed

76
00:02:42,199 --> 00:02:45,180
to ask that person to open the bottle

77
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:46,759
for me? Or let's say for example if

78
00:02:46,759 --> 00:02:48,900
I'm part of a Chassidish community where they

79
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:50,900
end Shabbos with the zman of Rabbeinu Tam

80
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,120
and they keep Shabbos very late and they

81
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:54,520
have a long Shalosh Seudos. And now it's

82
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:55,780
an hour and a half after Shabbos, is

83
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,680
over but they're still going. Would I be

84
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,340
allowed to ask that person to do something

85
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:02,120
for me when I, as a person who's

86
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:03,919
not a member of the community, has already

87
00:03:03,919 --> 00:03:06,520
ended Shabbos and said Havdalah? All of those

88
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:08,300
are very important questions and I don't think

89
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,759
the answer is going to be the same

90
00:03:09,759 --> 00:03:11,865
in all of those cases. Yeah, I think

91
00:03:11,865 --> 00:03:14,015
this is different. I know someone in my

92
00:03:14,015 --> 00:03:16,164
community who, like we have a person there

93
00:03:16,164 --> 00:03:18,582
who's a Ben-Torah, doesn't follow eruv, so like

94
00:03:18,582 --> 00:03:21,000
his mom will carry his tallis. Aha, so

95
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,139
that's a very interesting story you raised, because

96
00:03:23,139 --> 00:03:24,939
there's a story about Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach

97
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,659
that there was a boy in Yerushalayim who

98
00:03:26,659 --> 00:03:29,460
lived in Shaarei Chesed who decided for himself

99
00:03:29,460 --> 00:03:30,719
that he was not going to use the

100
00:03:30,719 --> 00:03:33,400
eruv. So one Shabbos Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach

101
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,020
notices that the boy is walking down the

102
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:37,180
street together with his father. His father was

103
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:38,800
in his 70s, was holding a big stack

104
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:40,240
of chairs, and the son is walking right

105
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:42,120
next to him. So Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach

106
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:43,460
stops him in the street and he says:

107
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:45,360
What's going on? Your father's 70 years old,

108
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,479
you're 25. How come your father's carrying the

109
00:03:47,479 --> 00:03:49,099
stack of chairs? He says: Well we have

110
00:03:49,099 --> 00:03:50,379
a kiddush down the block and they needed

111
00:03:50,379 --> 00:03:51,879
extra chairs so we went home, we got

112
00:03:51,879 --> 00:03:54,680
some chairs. So Rav Shlomo Zalman said: No,

113
00:03:54,699 --> 00:03:56,020
I'm not asking where you're going with the

114
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:58,020
chairs. I'm asking why are you allowing your

115
00:03:58,020 --> 00:03:59,680
father to carry the chairs instead of you?

116
00:04:00,039 --> 00:04:01,319
And he said: Well I don't carry in

117
00:04:01,319 --> 00:04:02,979
the eruv. So he said: Because you decided

118
00:04:02,979 --> 00:04:04,419
you want to be machmir and not carry

119
00:04:04,419 --> 00:04:05,780
in the eruv, you think you're allowed to

120
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:08,060
violate kibbud av va'em and make your father

121
00:04:08,060 --> 00:04:11,060
schlepp the chairs for you? So it's a

122
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:12,699
difficult balance. And by the way, that's something

123
00:04:12,699 --> 00:04:14,039
that you have to think about in general:

124
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:17,439
Every chumra that you take upon yourself, chumras

125
00:04:17,439 --> 00:04:19,160
are very nice, but every chumra that you

126
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:21,959
take upon yourself has a price that you're

127
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:24,439
going to have to pay for it. That's

128
00:04:24,439 --> 00:04:25,839
the way it is in life. There's a

129
00:04:25,839 --> 00:04:29,860
trade-off, right? So let's say for example, a

130
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:32,339
chumra that we're familiar with is the Gemara

131
00:04:32,339 --> 00:04:34,720
says in Hilchos Niddah, in Maseches Niddah, that

132
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:36,680
women today took upon themselves that they are

133
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:38,579
more machmir in Hilchos Niddah than they were

134
00:04:38,579 --> 00:04:39,800
in the days of the Mishnah. They wait

135
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:41,428
longer than they otherwise would have had to.

136
00:04:41,428 --> 00:04:43,180
Whatever, it's a you'll get there when you

137
00:04:43,180 --> 00:04:45,240
get there, but that's that's the halakhah. We

138
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,899
are machmir way, way beyond what the Torah

139
00:04:47,899 --> 00:04:50,079
expects us to do. Now, you'll say it's

140
00:04:50,079 --> 00:04:52,180
beautiful, taharas hamishpacha, it's so nice, it brings

141
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:55,300
kedushah into your house, it's wonderful. Yeah, but

142
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:56,680
you're also paying a price for that. What

143
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:59,540
price are you paying? What price are you

144
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:02,199
paying? There's also a mitzvah for a husband

145
00:05:02,199 --> 00:05:04,139
and wife to be married to each other

146
00:05:04,139 --> 00:05:06,079
and to be interacting with one another and

147
00:05:06,079 --> 00:05:08,879
to be having responsibilities toward one another in

148
00:05:08,879 --> 00:05:12,819
physical ways, so you're again, the Gemara assumes

149
00:05:12,819 --> 00:05:15,019
that that's a fair trade-off, but understand that

150
00:05:15,019 --> 00:05:18,060
every chumra that is instituted always has a

151
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,220
price that you have to pay on the

152
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:20,519
other side and you really have to think

153
00:05:20,519 --> 00:05:23,480
carefully is this really worthwhile? Is this something

154
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:24,980
that is a price that is worthy of

155
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:26,720
paying for the chumra that I want to

156
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:28,399
do? So that's an important question. So here

157
00:05:29,180 --> 00:05:31,639
the question begins let's say by Shabbos When

158
00:05:31,639 --> 00:05:33,879
I'm mekabbel Shabbos early would I be allowed

159
00:05:33,879 --> 00:05:35,740
to ask somebody who has not yet accepted

160
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:37,899
Shabbos to do a melacha on my behalf?

161
00:05:37,959 --> 00:05:39,480
I come home from shul Friday night, I

162
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:40,740
see that the lights are not on, the

163
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:42,540
air conditioner is not set. Would I be

164
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:44,319
allowed to ask my neighbor when it's still

165
00:05:44,319 --> 00:05:46,540
light outside to then come into my home

166
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:47,899
and do everything that needs to be done?

167
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:50,740
Good question. Okay. So we go to a

168
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:53,300
Gemara Maseches Shabbos. The Gemara says that I'm

169
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,100
not allowed to hire workers to do work

170
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:56,439
for me on Shabbos. Now what does that

171
00:05:56,439 --> 00:05:58,019
mean? It means if I have a field

172
00:05:58,019 --> 00:05:59,399
and I need people to work the field,

173
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:00,720
I'm not allowed to have people work the

174
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:02,800
field. It also means, says the Gemara, that

175
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,259
I'm not allowed to have someone if let's

176
00:06:04,259 --> 00:06:06,899
say I have a field in Kansas. Now

177
00:06:07,259 --> 00:06:08,519
I'm not going to have anybody work the

178
00:06:08,519 --> 00:06:10,160
field but I'm afraid, I live in New

179
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:11,339
York and I'm not going to be there

180
00:06:11,339 --> 00:06:14,208
for Shabbos so I'm concerned that maybe someone

181
00:06:14,208 --> 00:06:16,560
is going to come steal all my produce

182
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:17,939
on Shabbos. so I need to have somebody

183
00:06:17,939 --> 00:06:20,279
watching over the field. Says the Gemara you're

184
00:06:20,279 --> 00:06:21,600
not allowed to have a worker. That's also

185
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:24,199
called work. But the Gemara says I'm allowed

186
00:06:24,199 --> 00:06:26,379
to make a deal with another Jew who

187
00:06:26,379 --> 00:06:28,420
happens to live in Kansas right near my

188
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:30,519
field. What's the deal? He owns a piece

189
00:06:30,519 --> 00:06:32,480
of property in New York right near my

190
00:06:32,480 --> 00:06:34,339
house. I own a piece of property in

191
00:06:34,339 --> 00:06:36,360
Kansas right near his house. I'm allowed to

192
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:37,694
say to him shmor li ve'eshmor lach. You

193
00:06:37,694 --> 00:06:41,000
watch over my field over Shabbos, I'll watch

194
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,519
over your piece of property here in New

195
00:06:42,519 --> 00:06:44,300
York over Shabbos and everybody will be fine.

196
00:06:44,699 --> 00:06:46,379
I'm not paying you, you're not paying me,

197
00:06:46,699 --> 00:06:48,319
we're each doing each other a favor that's

198
00:06:48,319 --> 00:06:51,000
mutually beneficial. So the Gemara says that that

199
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,699
would be permissible. Now why is that permissible?

200
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:56,279
In the end of the day he's doing

201
00:06:56,279 --> 00:06:58,459
the work for you. Along comes the Rashba,

202
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,579
the Rashba is one of the great Rishonim,

203
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,279
and the Rashba says that the reason for

204
00:07:03,279 --> 00:07:04,893
that is because kelomar af al pi shehu

205
00:07:04,893 --> 00:07:09,139
eino yachol lelech lesham, even though I'm in

206
00:07:09,139 --> 00:07:10,980
New York right now and I'm unable to

207
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:12,639
be in Kansas, I wouldn't be able to

208
00:07:12,639 --> 00:07:14,500
walk there on Shabbos, it's too far, it's

209
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:16,959
out of the techum, says the Rashba, since

210
00:07:16,959 --> 00:07:19,500
theoretically I would be able to figure out

211
00:07:19,500 --> 00:07:22,259
a way to be there, therefore it's not

212
00:07:22,259 --> 00:07:24,259
considered to be a problem because I myself

213
00:07:24,259 --> 00:07:25,319
could have figured out a way to be

214
00:07:25,319 --> 00:07:26,779
there. Had I set up a hut right

215
00:07:26,779 --> 00:07:28,399
next to my field, I would have been

216
00:07:28,399 --> 00:07:29,800
able to watch it myself. Since I could

217
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:32,279
have done it myself theoretically, I'm allowed to

218
00:07:32,279 --> 00:07:33,720
have someone else doing it. Says the Rashba,

219
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:35,820
and from here we learn de-Yisrael she-kibel alav

220
00:07:35,820 --> 00:07:40,459
Shabbos kodem she-chashecha, a Jew that accepts Shabbos

221
00:07:40,459 --> 00:07:43,045
early before it's dark outside, mutar lomar le-Yisrael

222
00:07:43,045 --> 00:07:46,500
chaveiro la'asos lo melacha plonis. I'm allowed to

223
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,100
go to my friend who has not yet

224
00:07:48,100 --> 00:07:50,519
accepted Shabbos, and I'm allowed to ask that

225
00:07:50,519 --> 00:07:52,980
person to do a melacha for me. Why?

226
00:07:53,259 --> 00:07:55,439
Why? What's the logic? Finish off the sentence.

227
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:59,259
How is that similar to the case of

228
00:07:59,259 --> 00:08:05,660
the Gemara? Was

229
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:08,860
I not clear? The end of the sentence

230
00:08:08,860 --> 00:08:11,199
is because I would have had the opportunity,

231
00:08:11,319 --> 00:08:13,339
had I chosen not to accept Shabbos early,

232
00:08:13,379 --> 00:08:15,319
I could have done melacha myself. So I

233
00:08:15,319 --> 00:08:16,620
made a decision this week that I want

234
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:19,100
to keep Shabbos early, but since theoretically I

235
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:20,939
could have done the melacha myself, therefore I'm

236
00:08:20,939 --> 00:08:22,680
allowed to ask a Jew to do the

237
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:24,779
melacha who hasn't yet accepted Shabbos which is

238
00:08:24,779 --> 00:08:27,000
similar, says the Rashba, to that case of

239
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,720
the Gemara where had I set up shop

240
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:30,519
in Kansas I would have been able to

241
00:08:30,519 --> 00:08:32,240
watch over my field myself and therefore I'm

242
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:34,019
allowed to ask the person who's a resident

243
00:08:34,019 --> 00:08:36,399
there to watch over it for me. That's

244
00:08:36,399 --> 00:08:39,379
the association that he makes. The Ran, one

245
00:08:39,379 --> 00:08:41,740
of the other Rishonim, disagrees, but the Shulchan

246
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:44,080
Aruch accepts the opinion of the Rashba. And

247
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:46,539
he says exactly what the Rashba says, that

248
00:08:46,539 --> 00:08:49,700
if I accept Shabbos early I'd be allowed

249
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:52,679
to then go ahead and ask somebody, if

250
00:08:52,679 --> 00:08:53,840
I come home and I see the lights

251
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,080
are not on, I'd be allowed to ask

252
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,779
somebody to come and do the melacha on

253
00:08:57,779 --> 00:09:00,139
my behalf because they have not yet accepted

254
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:02,460
Shabbos, and that would be acceptable. A lot

255
00:09:02,460 --> 00:09:04,179
to talk about in that Rashba, a lot

256
00:09:04,179 --> 00:09:06,279
to understand, but just on a very basic

257
00:09:06,279 --> 00:09:08,080
simple level the Shulchan Aruch quotes the Rashba

258
00:09:08,519 --> 00:09:09,919
that that would be permissible. So let me

259
00:09:09,919 --> 00:09:12,440
give you a fascinating extension that the Pri

260
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:14,259
Megadim writes in Shulchan Aruch. Pri Megadim says,

261
00:09:14,259 --> 00:09:16,820
a kohen is not allowed to go into

262
00:09:16,820 --> 00:09:19,799
a cemetery. Not only is he not allowed

263
00:09:19,799 --> 00:09:21,200
to go into a cemetery, he's not allowed

264
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:22,659
to go into a house that has a

265
00:09:22,659 --> 00:09:25,299
dead body in it either. Right? It's a

266
00:09:25,299 --> 00:09:26,659
big question whether a kohen is allowed to

267
00:09:26,659 --> 00:09:29,379
visit a hospital. Every hospital has dead bodies

268
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:32,340
unfortunately every day. So is a kohen allowed

269
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:33,659
to go into a hospital where he knows

270
00:09:33,659 --> 00:09:35,960
that by entering the building he's probably going

271
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:37,779
to come not in contact with a dead

272
00:09:37,779 --> 00:09:39,559
body, but somewhere under the roof of the

273
00:09:39,559 --> 00:09:40,879
building there's going to be a dead body.

274
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:42,960
These are all questions you have to think

275
00:09:42,960 --> 00:09:45,259
about, but now you'll say you don't have

276
00:09:45,259 --> 00:09:49,139
to think about them, why? Why? Because even

277
00:09:49,139 --> 00:09:51,860
if you are from a family of kohanim,

278
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:55,460
there's no prohibition on women to become tamei,

279
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:58,259
right? Female kohanim, any kohanim in the room?

280
00:09:59,179 --> 00:10:02,559
No? Oh, so you're allowed to go to

281
00:10:02,559 --> 00:10:04,460
a cemetery. You shouldn't have to, but you're

282
00:10:04,460 --> 00:10:06,440
allowed to go to a cemetery. The prohibition

283
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:08,139
is only on male Kohanim. So you'll say,

284
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:09,559
so why am I even mentioning this about

285
00:10:09,559 --> 00:10:13,340
the hospital? You know why? Im yirtzeh Hashem,

286
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:16,033
someday you're going to be pregnant. Im yirtzeh

287
00:10:16,033 --> 00:10:17,360
Hashem, it's possible that you're going to be

288
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:19,340
pregnant with a boy. My wife did it

289
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:22,399
six times with boys. We specialize in boys.

290
00:10:22,639 --> 00:10:26,220
So this is a big shayla. If you

291
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:29,080
are pregnant with a boy, are you allowed

292
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:30,960
to go to a cemetery or is that

293
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:32,740
considered as if you are bringing a male

294
00:10:32,740 --> 00:10:35,899
Kohein into a cemetery? How do you like

295
00:10:35,899 --> 00:10:42,620
that? Interesting. That is a question. So

296
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:44,580
I get the question all the time from

297
00:10:44,580 --> 00:10:46,740
pregnant women who are not from a family

298
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:48,480
of Kohanim. Am I allowed to go to

299
00:10:48,480 --> 00:10:49,559
a funeral, am I allowed to go to

300
00:10:49,559 --> 00:10:51,139
a cemetery? The answer to that is yes.

301
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:52,940
Some say al pi Kabbalah, whatever it is,

302
00:10:53,019 --> 00:10:55,740
but the halacha says it's absolutely permissible for

303
00:10:55,740 --> 00:10:57,080
a woman who is pregnant to go to

304
00:10:57,080 --> 00:10:58,899
a cemetery or to attend a levayah, there's

305
00:10:58,899 --> 00:11:00,639
no problem at all. What they don't know

306
00:11:00,639 --> 00:11:02,519
to ask is if I am married to

307
00:11:02,519 --> 00:11:04,539
a Kohein and the child that I am

308
00:11:04,539 --> 00:11:07,100
now pregnant with is a boy, am I

309
00:11:07,100 --> 00:11:08,779
allowed to go to a cemetery? That is

310
00:11:08,779 --> 00:11:10,919
a real serious question. That is a major

311
00:11:10,919 --> 00:11:12,919
shayla that is discussed in the Shach and

312
00:11:12,919 --> 00:11:15,279
all the other poskim. It's a big big

313
00:11:15,279 --> 00:11:17,080
shayla, not for now, but that's the shayla.

314
00:11:17,220 --> 00:11:19,279
So we know that a Kohein, a male

315
00:11:19,279 --> 00:11:21,840
Kohein, is not allowed to go and come

316
00:11:21,840 --> 00:11:23,600
in contact with a dead body. Says the

317
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,539
Pri Megadim, would a Kohein be allowed to

318
00:11:25,539 --> 00:11:27,379
ask somebody, let's say I have my suitcase

319
00:11:27,379 --> 00:11:29,419
that got stuck in somebody's house and now

320
00:11:29,419 --> 00:11:30,440
there's a dead body in the house, but

321
00:11:30,440 --> 00:11:31,460
I need the suitcase, I gotta go to

322
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:32,440
the airport, so what do I do? Of

323
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:33,679
course I can't go in the house myself.

324
00:11:34,059 --> 00:11:35,539
Can I ask somebody else to go in

325
00:11:35,539 --> 00:11:37,299
the house for me and take it out

326
00:11:37,299 --> 00:11:41,200
on my behalf? So the Pri Megadim writes

327
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:44,317
as follows, Lefi zeh, mutar lekohein lomar leYisrael

328
00:11:44,317 --> 00:11:47,700
leilech lemakom tumat meis ulehavi lo misham heftzo.

329
00:11:47,700 --> 00:11:51,340
I'm allowed to go in, I'm sorry, I

330
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:52,659
can't go in myself, and I'm allowed to

331
00:11:52,659 --> 00:11:55,320
instruct a Kohein to go in for me.

332
00:11:55,659 --> 00:11:58,360
And he learns that from this halacha that

333
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:00,470
the Shulchan Aruch says. Just like the Shulchan

334
00:12:00,470 --> 00:12:02,759
Aruch says, when I was not mekabbel Shabbos

335
00:12:02,779 --> 00:12:04,923
but you were, I'm allowed to ask you

336
00:12:04,923 --> 00:12:07,072
to do some activity for me that I

337
00:12:07,072 --> 00:12:09,221
myself am not allowed to do, so too

338
00:12:09,221 --> 00:12:11,700
here, he says, if a Kohein is going

339
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:13,080
to be instructed to go in and get

340
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:14,580
the Kohein's suitcase from the house that has

341
00:12:14,580 --> 00:12:16,039
a dead body in it, that would be

342
00:12:16,039 --> 00:12:17,799
something that is permissible because he sees that

343
00:12:17,799 --> 00:12:20,980
as an extension of this halacha. What about

344
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:22,740
in the other direction? I'll take your question

345
00:12:22,740 --> 00:12:24,559
in a minute. Says the Rama, what about

346
00:12:24,559 --> 00:12:26,039
in the other direction? Like we said, you're

347
00:12:26,039 --> 00:12:28,389
from a Hasidish community and you keep Rabbeinu

348
00:12:28,389 --> 00:12:31,120
Tam and even beyond Rabbeinu Tam, your Shalosh

349
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:33,059
Seudos goes for another two hours after Shabbos

350
00:12:33,059 --> 00:12:35,000
is over. Many of the communities do that,

351
00:12:35,259 --> 00:12:38,220
right? So what happens in that interim period?

352
00:12:38,220 --> 00:12:41,480
If you're from my community, you've made Havdalah,

353
00:12:41,720 --> 00:12:44,600
the second Shabbos is over. Now down the

354
00:12:44,600 --> 00:12:46,370
block we have Hasidim who are having Shalosh

355
00:12:46,370 --> 00:12:48,220
Seudos and the lights go out. Can they

356
00:12:48,220 --> 00:12:49,899
call me over and say Shechter, the lights

357
00:12:49,899 --> 00:12:51,279
went out, can you please turn them on?

358
00:12:51,580 --> 00:12:53,279
Says the Rama, the same way that you're

359
00:12:53,279 --> 00:12:54,600
allowed to do it at the beginning of

360
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:57,460
Shabbos that if I was mekabbel Shabbos, early

361
00:12:57,519 --> 00:12:58,740
I'm allowed to go to my neighbor and

362
00:12:58,740 --> 00:13:00,539
ask them to turn the lights on, so

363
00:13:00,539 --> 00:13:01,919
too says the Rama at the end of

364
00:13:01,919 --> 00:13:04,460
Shabbos on the other end, if I decide

365
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:06,139
that I want to observe Shabbos longer than

366
00:13:06,139 --> 00:13:08,899
I have to, I'm allowed to call somebody

367
00:13:08,899 --> 00:13:10,879
else over to my house to do a

368
00:13:10,879 --> 00:13:13,080
melachah that I myself would not be allowed

369
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:14,440
to do yet because I did not make

370
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:16,740
Havdalah. That is what the Rama makes the

371
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:19,200
extension, which seems like a natural extension from

372
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:21,259
what the Shulchan Aruch says. Along comes the

373
00:13:21,259 --> 00:13:24,639
Levush. The Levush lives in the 1500s, same

374
00:13:24,639 --> 00:13:26,128
time as the Beis Yosef and the Rama,

375
00:13:26,128 --> 00:13:27,899
and he says no no no, before Shabbos

376
00:13:27,899 --> 00:13:29,240
is not the same as after Shabbos, and

377
00:13:29,240 --> 00:13:31,159
he makes a whole distinction. The bottom line

378
00:13:31,159 --> 00:13:32,620
is the Rama says that it would be

379
00:13:32,620 --> 00:13:33,879
the same. And the same way you're allowed

380
00:13:33,879 --> 00:13:35,960
to ask before Shabbos, so too you are

381
00:13:35,960 --> 00:13:37,799
allowed to ask at the end of Shabbos,

382
00:13:37,899 --> 00:13:39,539
which now leads us to the next question,

383
00:13:39,659 --> 00:13:42,100
which is what about when it comes to

384
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:44,159
Yom Tov Sheni? Would all of this apply

385
00:13:44,159 --> 00:13:46,059
to Yom Tov Sheni as well or would

386
00:13:46,059 --> 00:13:47,786
you say no, the halacha by Yom Tov

387
00:13:47,786 --> 00:13:50,200
Sheni would be a completely different story? This

388
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:54,220
is a discussion that is had in the

389
00:13:54,220 --> 00:13:57,980
Birkei Yosef. Birkei Yosef is the Chida, who

390
00:13:57,980 --> 00:14:00,679
quotes two of the great Sefardish Gedolim who

391
00:14:00,679 --> 00:14:03,740
disagreed about this point. I'm going to read

392
00:14:03,740 --> 00:14:05,533
you what he says. Ben chutz la'aretz she'omed

393
00:14:05,533 --> 00:14:07,720
be'eretz Yisrael. If you have somebody who is

394
00:14:07,720 --> 00:14:09,559
a resident of chutz la'aretz but is spending

395
00:14:09,559 --> 00:14:12,700
Yom Tov in eretz Yisrael. Katav haMaharikash. He

396
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:15,059
quotes from one of the Gedolim who said,

397
00:14:15,059 --> 00:14:17,139
shemutar lo lomar le-ben eretz Yisrael sheya'aseh lo

398
00:14:17,139 --> 00:14:20,000
melachah be-Yom Tov Sheni. He first quotes the

399
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,820
Maharikash who said just like by erev Shabbos

400
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:24,620
if I accepted Shabbos early I'd be allowed

401
00:14:24,620 --> 00:14:26,019
to ask someone to do melachah for me,

402
00:14:26,399 --> 00:14:27,620
so too here I'd be allowed to do

403
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:29,580
the same. On the other hand, he then

404
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:32,019
quotes other acharonim who say that that would

405
00:14:32,019 --> 00:14:33,940
not be permissible and he says the Mahari

406
00:14:33,940 --> 00:14:35,519
Molcho, I don't know who that is, but

407
00:14:35,519 --> 00:14:36,860
he quotes him and if the Birkei Yosef

408
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:38,799
quotes him he must be someone important. So

409
00:14:38,799 --> 00:14:41,480
he says, dacha devarav vehiskimu le'esor, and he

410
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:43,240
says that that would be something that we

411
00:14:43,240 --> 00:14:45,220
would not permit, that would not be allowed

412
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:49,100
to be done on Yom Tov Sheni. Why?

413
00:14:49,399 --> 00:14:50,840
Why should it be different? Why should we

414
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:54,480
distinguish between erev Shabbos and Yom Tov Sheni?

415
00:14:55,679 --> 00:14:57,019
So that's a big question that you need

416
00:14:57,019 --> 00:14:58,379
to think about. But it seems that the

417
00:14:58,379 --> 00:15:00,980
consensus of the Gedolei HaPoskim. is that even

418
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:02,679
though when you make early Shabbos, you'd be

419
00:15:02,679 --> 00:15:04,080
allowed to ask someone to do a melacha

420
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:05,340
for you that you would not be allowed

421
00:15:05,340 --> 00:15:07,159
to do. And even though the Rama says

422
00:15:07,159 --> 00:15:08,519
on the back end, you'd be allowed to

423
00:15:08,519 --> 00:15:10,620
ask someone after Shabbos is over to do

424
00:15:10,620 --> 00:15:12,279
melacha, even though it's still Shabbos for you.

425
00:15:12,539 --> 00:15:13,940
When it comes to Yom Tov Sheini, it

426
00:15:13,940 --> 00:15:15,279
would seem that you would not be allowed

427
00:15:15,279 --> 00:15:16,980
to do that and it's not necessarily the

428
00:15:16,980 --> 00:15:19,299
same. We don't say that these two situations

429
00:15:19,299 --> 00:15:21,220
would be the same. This came up with

430
00:15:21,220 --> 00:15:24,700
a major, major shaila when it was a

431
00:15:24,700 --> 00:15:26,986
get that had to be performed on Yom

432
00:15:26,986 --> 00:15:30,259
Tov Sheini. A get had to be written

433
00:15:30,259 --> 00:15:31,639
on Yom Tov Sheini. Now, what does it

434
00:15:31,639 --> 00:15:32,659
mean a get has to be written on

435
00:15:32,659 --> 00:15:34,159
Yom Tov Sheini? Get divorced the next day.

436
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:36,080
What's the big deal? The answer is sometimes

437
00:15:36,080 --> 00:15:37,659
you have a husband who's giving a very

438
00:15:37,659 --> 00:15:40,299
difficult time and he's not prepared to give

439
00:15:40,299 --> 00:15:43,220
his wife a get, Rachmana Litzlan, and he's

440
00:15:43,220 --> 00:15:45,019
leaving her an agunah, and he says, well,

441
00:15:45,019 --> 00:15:46,559
tomorrow morning I have a flight, I'm going

442
00:15:46,559 --> 00:15:48,039
to the Netherlands and I'm never coming back.

443
00:15:48,860 --> 00:15:50,059
And that's it, we're not going to find

444
00:15:50,059 --> 00:15:52,279
him anymore. So, it's Yom Tov Sheini, we

445
00:15:52,279 --> 00:15:53,840
have to figure out what to do, but

446
00:15:53,840 --> 00:15:55,679
the problem is we don't really have the

447
00:15:55,679 --> 00:15:58,053
ability to write the get because it's Yom

448
00:15:58,053 --> 00:16:00,159
Tov Sheini for us. So can we have

449
00:16:00,159 --> 00:16:02,100
a Ben Eretz Yisrael write a get for

450
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:04,299
us on Yom Tov Sheini when for us,

451
00:16:04,299 --> 00:16:06,000
we are Bnei Chutz La'aretz but we're in

452
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,220
Eretz Yisrael now? We're observing the second day

453
00:16:08,220 --> 00:16:09,799
of Yom Tov. Would we be allowed to

454
00:16:09,799 --> 00:16:11,539
do that or perhaps not? That's a major,

455
00:16:11,700 --> 00:16:14,539
major interesting discussion in the poskim about that.

456
00:16:15,419 --> 00:16:17,100
But the bottom line is that it would

457
00:16:17,100 --> 00:16:19,059
seem that the consensus would be that you

458
00:16:19,059 --> 00:16:20,419
would not be allowed to ask someone on

459
00:16:20,419 --> 00:16:22,399
Yom Tov Sheini to do melacha for you

460
00:16:22,399 --> 00:16:24,740
unless Rav Shlomo Zalman writes unless it's a

461
00:16:24,740 --> 00:16:27,379
major makom tzorech, unless it's a major, major

462
00:16:27,379 --> 00:16:30,100
emergency, something really important that needs to be

463
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:32,519
done, makom mitzvah. But outside of that, it

464
00:16:32,519 --> 00:16:33,779
would seem that this is something that is

465
00:16:33,779 --> 00:16:36,340
certainly not advisable and certainly not something that

466
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:40,519
we would encourage people to be involved with.

467
00:16:41,240 --> 00:16:43,440
The last thing that we don't have enough

468
00:16:43,440 --> 00:16:45,279
time to talk about is if I'm machmir

469
00:16:45,279 --> 00:16:46,940
on a Shabbos and I decide that I

470
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:48,940
don't want to do opening bottles or something

471
00:16:48,940 --> 00:16:50,840
like that carrying the eiruv, would I be

472
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:52,600
allowed to ask somebody else to do it

473
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:54,960
on my behalf when I myself would not

474
00:16:54,960 --> 00:16:57,159
do it? But we'll save that maybe.
